 REACTION MODELING IN FAST NEUTRON RANGE                          2631 1451
 ---------------------------------------                          2631 1451
                                                                  2631 1451
 The fast region calculations were performed using the EMPIRE     2631 1451
 code, which is a comprehensive package that integrates into a    2631 1451
 single system a number of important physical modules and         2631 1451
 evaluation tools. We have adopted coupled channel (CC)           2631 1451
 calculations in the incident channel and for the direct          2631 1451
 inelastic scattering to collective levels. In addition, we have  2631 1451
 allowed for the Distorted Wave Born Approximation (DWBA) to      2631 1451
 populate presumably collective levels which are not included in  2631 1451
 the ground state band used in the CC calculations to adjust      2631 1451
 neutron emission spectra in the high energy range. The           2631 1451
 sophisticated optical potential used for the CC calculations on  2631 1451
 56Fe was not designed for incident neutrons with energies below  2631 1451
 4 MeV. In order to match averaged experimental cross sections,   2631 1451
 the CC predictions were scaled down by a factor of 0.78 from 1   2631 1451
 keV to 0.5 MeV and later modulated by factor varying between     2631 1451
 0.68 and 0.9 up to 2.5 MeV and eventually increased to gradually 2631 1451
 reach 1.0 at 4 MeV . This had very little impact on the          2631 1451
 evaluation since practically all major cross sections below 4    2631 1451
 MeV were determined by the measurements.                         2631 1451
                                                                  2631 1451
 For the preequilibrium (PE) emission employed the classical      2631 1451
 exciton model (PCROSS module) because of its higher flexibility  2631 1451
 in modeling neutron spectra compared with the physically more    2631 1451
 advanced, but also more rigid quantum mechanical models. The     2631 1451
 same module was also used for the PE emission of protons and     2631 1451
 gammas as well as for PE emission of clusters, the latter taking 2631 1451
 advantage of the Iwamoto-Harada model.                           2631 1451
                                                                  2631 1451
 The Hauser-Feshbach formulation of the statistical model was     2631 1451
 employed to follow sequential emission of particles from the     2631 1451
 compound nucleus (CN). The elastic and inelastic angular         2631 1451
 distributions of neutrons emitted from the CN were calculated    2631 1451
 using the Blatt-Biedenharn formalism. The width fluctuation      2631 1451
 factor was accounted for within HRTW formalism applied up to     2631 1451
 12.0 MeV incident neutrons. Compound nucleus and direct cross    2631 1451
 sections were added incoherently.                                2631 1451
                                                                  2631 1451
 Gilbert-Cameron model for level-density has been adopted due to  2631 1451
 it performance on the 56Fe(n,p) reaction that is extremely       2631 1451
 sensitive to level densities. Level density parameters were      2631 1451
 fitted to reproduce the asymptotic behavior of discrete levels,  2631 1451
 as well as to correctly describe the observed cross sections.    2631 1451
                                                                  2631 1451
 Complete g-ray cascades for every excited nucleus were           2631 1451
 calculated using M1, E1 and E2 electromagnetic transitions.      2631 1451
 g-strength functions for E1 transitions from the RIPL3 MLO1      2631 1451
 formulation by Pluyko (the only exception was made for 56Fe      2631 1451
 where scaled Weisskopf single particle estimates were used).     2631 1451
                                                                  2631 1451
 Occasionally, we resorted to energy-dependent parameter scaling  2631 1451
 or tuning factors, available in the EMPIRE code, to account for  2631 1451
 model deficiencies. These corrections were typically on the      2631 1451
 level of 1-2% or less and can be found in the input files.       2631 1451
                                                                  2631 1451
 FAST NEUTRON RANGE                                               2631 1451
 ------------------                                               2631 1451
                                                                  2631 1451
 MF=3  Neutron cross sections                                     2631 1451
                                                                  2631 1451
   EMPIRE calculations adjusted to reproduce experimental data    2631 1451
   were adopted unless stated differently below.                  2631 1451
                                                                  2631 1451
   MT=1 Total                                                     2631 1451
     The adopted cross sections above the resonance range up to 102631 1451
     MeV were the taken from JEFF- 3.2, which is essentially the  2631 1451
     Vonach-Tagesen evaluation with superimposed fluctuations that2631 1451
     correspond to the rescaled Berthold measurement. Above 10 MeV2631 1451
     the total cross sections were provided by coupled channel    2631 1451
     calculations with the soft-rotor dispersive potential by     2631 1451
     Soukhovitski-Capote with minor adjustments (factor of 0.997  2631 1451
     up to 20 MeV and less than +1.5% above) to reproduce the     2631 1451
     measurement by Abfalterer.                                   2631 1451
                                                                  2631 1451
   MT=2 Elastic scattering                                        2631 1451
     The elastic cross section is defined as the difference       2631 1451
     between the total and the remaining partial cross sections.  2631 1451
     To test the consistency, the cross section was resolution    2631 1451
     -broadened to 0.3 percent, achieving good agreement with     2631 1451
     Kinney data (although some underestimation was noted).       2631 1451
                                                                  2631 1451
   MT=4, 51-91 Inelastic scattering                               2631 1451
     JEFF-3.2 contains inelastic cross sections measured by Dupont2631 1451
     et al., but the authors discovered normalisation problems and2631 1451
     the results were never published. New measurements were      2631 1451
     performed by Negret, but with a lower resolution. The Dupont 2631 1451
     and Negret data were binned over a suitable energy mesh.     2631 1451
     A piecewise linear scaling parameter was applied to          2631 1451
     Dupont data such that they agree on average with the Negret  2631 1451
     data. It was also found that the energy calibration of Negret2631 1451
     data did not match the resonances of the total cross section.2631 1451
     A correction was made to the energy scale which amounted to  2631 1451
     2.5 keV at 1.8 MeV.                                          2631 1451
     In addition, the inelastic cross section below 1 MeV was     2631 1451
     reduced by 15 percent to approximately agree with Perey data.2631 1451
     The evaluation reproduces very well Negret data up to about  2631 1451
     7 MeV and then follows the results of Nelson. In the plateau 2631 1451
     of the inelastic cross section no adjustment of the model    2631 1451
     parameters was needed.                                       2631 1451
     Fluctuations were imposed on the inelastic scattering to the 2631 1451
     first and the second excited state in 56Fe up to 2.5 MeV     2631 1451
     following the rescaled Dupont data. Between 2.5 and 3.5 MeV  2631 1451
     the lower resolution Negret fluctuations were applied. Smooth2631 1451
     EMPIRE calculations were adopted above 3.5 MeV for the first 2631 1451
     two inelastic levels and in the whole energy range for the   2631 1451
     remaining levels.                                            2631 1451
                                                                  2631 1451
   MT=5 (n,anything)                                              2631 1451
      Sum of all reactions not given explicitly in another MT     2631 1451
      number as calculated by the EMPIRE code. This MT includes   2631 1451
      majority of the reaction channels - namely all those for    2631 1451
      which we do not provide a complete set of exclusive emission2631 1451
      spectra.  The reason for this unusual representation is     2631 1451
      a difficulty to disentangle contributions of different      2631 1451
      reactions to the gamma spectrum from the final residue      2631 1451
      (e.g., gamma spectrum emitted from the decay of 55Mn        2631 1451
      calculated by EMPIRE is a sum of gammas emitted following   2631 1451
      (n,np), (n,pn) and (n,d) reactions). This becomes an issue  2631 1451
      at higher incident energies when multiple reactions         2631 1451
      contribute to the production of a given residue. Note that  2631 1451
      exclusive gamma emission spectrum is required by the ENDF-6 2631 1451
      format for each reaction that is treated as exclusive.      2631 1451
                                                                  2631 1451
      In the MT=5 representation, the cross sections can be       2631 1451
      obtained by multiplying cumulative cross section given      2631 1451
      in MT=5 by the relative yields for production of the 96     2631 1451
      residual nuclei given in the subsections of MF/MT=6/5.      2631 1451
      Particle and gamma emission spectra are summed up over      2631 1451
      the MT=5 reactions into the inclusive spectra given in      2631 1451
      the respective subsections of  MF/MT=6/5.                   2631 1451
                                                                  2631 1451
   MT=102 Capture                                                 2631 1451
     The capture cross sections above 860 keV were taken from     2631 1451
     the RPI data presented by Y. Danon at the ND2016 Conference. 2631 1451
                                                                  2631 1451
   MT=16   (n,2n)                                                 2631 1451
     EMPIRE caluclations adjusted to reproduce experimental data. 2631 1451
     Level densities and other parameters that impact 56Fe(n,2n)  2631 1451
     cross sections were fitted taking into account not only      2631 1451
     (n,2n) experimental data but also data for (n,p) and (n,a)   2631 1451
     reactions, in order to simultaneously improve the agreement  2631 1451
     with data for the three reactions. The most recent           2631 1451
     measurement by Wallner (NOT included in the fit) supports    2631 1451
     the new evaluation.                                          2631 1451
                                                                  2631 1451
   MT=17, 22, 28, 107, ...,849                                    2631 1451
     EMPIRE caluclations adjusted to reproduce experimental data. 2631 1451
     MTs NOT present! Included in MT=5.                           2631 1451
                                                                  2631 1451
